* Bash arrays
These examples use Bash. Indices start at 0; Zsh arrays normally start at 1.

* Create and append
@code bash
my_array=(1 2 "string with spaces" 4)
my_array+=("new value" "another value")
@end

* Loop over elements
Quote the expansion to preserve spaces and wildcard characters in each element.
@code bash
for element in "${my_array[@]}"; do
    printf '%s\n' "$element"
done
@end

* Loop over indices
@code bash
for index in "${!my_array[@]}"; do
    printf '%s: %s\n' "$index" "${my_array[$index]}"
done
@end

* Common operations
@code bash
# Empty array
arr=()
arr=(1 2 3)
# Third element, all elements, indices, and length
printf '%s\n' "${arr[2]}"
printf '%s\n' "${arr[@]}"
printf '%s\n' "${!arr[@]}"
printf '%s\n' "${#arr[@]}"
# Overwrite the first element and append
arr[0]=3
arr+=(4)
# Two elements starting at index 1
printf '%s\n' "${arr[@]:1:2}"
@end

* Collect filenames without parsing ls
Run in Bash; includes hidden entries and produces an empty array if there are no matches.
@code bash
(
    shopt -s nullglob dotglob
    files=(./*)
    for file in "${files[@]}"; do
        printf '%s\n' "$file"
    done
)
@end
The subshell keeps these options and variables local to the example.

Related: {:bash:}[bash], {:shell:}[shell].
